WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.389
A pathogen is a microorganism that causes disease. A microorganism is a living thing too small to see without a microscope. There are four types to know. Bacteria are single cells with no nucleus. Viruses are much smaller, and they can only reproduce inside our cells.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:34.118
A protozoan is a single cell with a nucleus. A fungus grows as thin threads. Not every microorganism is a pathogen. Many bacteria are harmless, so 'pathogen' is the word for all disease-causing organisms.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:50.887
Step one. Is it caught? A communicable disease is caused by a pathogen, and it can pass from one person to another. A non-communicable disease has no pathogen, so it cannot be caught. Diabetes and heart disease are examples.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:01:08.175
Step two. Learn which type causes each disease. Bacteria cause cholera, typhoid, tuberculosis and gonorrhoea. Viruses cause Ebola, AIDS, polio, influenza and hepatitis B. The virus that causes AIDS is called HIV.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:17.070
A protozoan causes malaria. A fungus causes athlete's foot. Write only one type of organism in each box of a table.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:30.898
Step four. Do not mix up the cause and the carrier. Malaria is caused by a protozoan called Plasmodium. A mosquito carries it from person to person. The mosquito is the vector, not the cause.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:46.167
Here is a real exam question. A passage says diabetes is a very common condition. The question asks: suggest why diabetes is described as a condition, rather than a disease. Two marks.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:56.222
Start with what makes a disease. A disease is caused by a pathogen, such as a bacterium or a virus. And it can pass from one person to another.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:07.552
Now diabetes. The passage says it comes from not making enough insulin, or not responding to it. There is no pathogen. So it cannot be caught, and it is a condition.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.929
Here is a full answer. It is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:27.191
A common mistake is to call all disease-causing organisms 'bacteria'. Many bacteria are harmless, and viruses, protozoa and fungi cause disease too. The general word is pathogen.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:40.315
Another mistake is to name the wrong thing. A mosquito does not cause malaria; a protozoan does. AIDS is a disease, not an organism; it is caused by HIV, a virus.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:58.495
Your turn. Ravi has cholera. Mei has hepatitis B. Sam has lung cancer, caused by smoking. Name the type of organism that causes Ravi's and Mei's illness. Then suggest why Sam's illness is non-communicable. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.507
Cholera is caused by a bacterium. Hepatitis B is caused by a virus.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:15.358
Sam's lung cancer is not caused by a pathogen. Smoking caused it. So it cannot be passed from one person to another: it is non-communicable.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:28.281
So, every time: a pathogen is a microorganism that causes disease. Communicable means it can be caught. Name one type of organism for each disease, and never the vector.
